      <div class="col-text">
        <p class="eyebrow">I · Foreword from the Advisory Committee</p>
        <p class="lede">It gives us great pleasure to present the 2025 Impact Report for Pela Terra Farmland — Fundo de Capital de Risco Fechado (‘Pela Terra Farmland’).</p>
        <hr class="rule" />
        <p>Impact is at the very heart of what we do. When we launched Pela Terra Farmland in 2021, our vision was clear: we want to create a world in which agriculture contributes to restoring our planet, rather than being a factor in its destruction.</p>
        <p>With many thousands of hectares of irrigated agricultural land, a climate ideally suited for mediterranean perennials, diverse agricultural traditions and a supportive regulatory environment, Portugal offers the perfect location to roll out pioneering, forward-looking agronomic approaches.<sup class="fnref"><a id="fnref-1" href="#fn-1">1</a></sup> Pela Terra Farmland leverages these advantages to offer a unique proposition: a golden visa-eligible investment that allows investors to invest in creating impact while earning European residency for themselves and their families.</p>
        <p>We believe that the imperative to give back is not simply a moral one: multiple studies have suggested that funds that pay close attention to Environmental, Social and Governance (ESG) factors perform better than their non-ESG peers.<sup class="fnref"><a id="fnref-2" href="#fn-2">2</a></sup><sup class="fnref"><a id="fnref-3" href="#fn-3">3</a></sup></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="boxout photo">
        <div class="img" style="background-image:url('/impactreports/2025/ptf/images/committee.jpg')"></div>
        <figcaption>Nathan Hadlock and Alex Lawry-White</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" data-section="I · Foreword" data-break>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="split-page reveal">
      <div class="col-text">
        <p>In agriculture, this relationship is clear on the ground: the adoption of regenerative agronomy correlates strongly to more resilient and consistent returns.<sup class="fnref"><a id="fnref-4" href="#fn-4">4</a></sup> It’s easy to see why — while conventional agriculture is in itself a significant contributor to the destruction of natural systems, agriculture as an industry is almost uniquely exposed to the risks related to environmental collapse. Changing weather patterns and collapsing biodiversity hit farmers before they hit other industries.</p>
        <p>In the five years since Pela Terra Farmland was launched, the environmental challenges facing our world have only become more acute. And as this report goes to press, ongoing geopolitical instability and resulting supply chain disruption is creating significant economic challenges for conventional farmers worldwide. The case for regenerative agriculture has never been stronger.</p>
        <p>Social and environmental impact is hugely important to Pela Terra Farmland. The goal is for the assets managed by the fund to serve as beacons of sustainable and regenerative good practice in Portugal and Europe more widely. However, we also want to go a step further: our approach is to act as a catalyst to drive impact at scale, leveraging both our own portfolio and strong relationships with partners and operators to drive change in the sector as a whole.</p>
        <p>We are excited to share this impact journey with readers of this report.</p>
        <p class="h-minor">— Nathan Hadlock and Alex Lawry-White</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="boxout quote">
        <span class="mark">“</span>
        <blockquote>For agriculture more than for any other industry, ‘doing the right thing’ correlates to stronger, more resilient returns.</blockquote>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
